Définition (source : International Financial Statistics database, International Monetary Fund (IMF);
World Development Indicators Database, World Bank (WB);
National Accounts data files, Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD)) : Domestic credit to private sector refers to financial resources provided to the private sector, such as through loans, purchases of nonequity securities, and trade credits and other accounts receivable, that establish a claim for repayment. For some countries these claims include credit to public enterprises. This indicator is expressed as a percentage of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) which is the total income earned through the production of goods and services in an economic territory during an accounting period.

